From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Data - bound Bezier InsertPoleAfter by the pole count
 the constructors accept

Geom2d_BezierCurve and Geom_BezierCurve disagree with themselves about how many
poles a Bezier curve may hold. Both constructors accept up to MaxDegree() + 1:

  if (nbpoles < 2 || nbpoles > (Geom2d_BezierCurve::MaxDegree() + 1))
    throw Standard_ConstructionError();

and Increase() tops out at a degree of MaxDegree(), which is the same bound
written as a degree. InsertPoleAfter compares the pole count against the degree
bound instead:

  Standard_ConstructionError_Raise_if(nbpoles >= Geom2d_BezierCurve::MaxDegree()
                                        || Weight <= gp::Resolution(), ...);

so insertion refuses once the curve holds MaxDegree() poles, two short of the
length the constructor would have built directly. A caller can hold a legally
constructed curve longer than InsertPoleAfter will grow a shorter one to.

MaxDegree() is a degree bound, not a pole bound: BSplCLib::MaxDegree() is 25,
and both classes size their static Multiplicities()/KnotSequence() tables as
MaxDegree() + 1 and index them by myPoles.Length() - 1, so a curve of
MaxDegree() + 1 poles is in range for every one of them. Comparing with > lets
insertion reach exactly the degree the constructors and Increase() allow and no
further.

Both sites in the package are changed, the 2d class and the 3d one; they are
the only two in the tree that compare a pole count against MaxDegree() with >=.
Geom_BezierSurface's InsertPoleColAfter/InsertPoleRowAfter carry no MaxDegree
bound at all, which is a different defect and is left alone here.

Reported as SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#2875.

diff --git a/src/ModelingData/TKG2d/Geom2d/Geom2d_BezierCurve.cxx b/src/ModelingData/TKG2d/Geom2d/Geom2d_BezierCurve.cxx
index 4ea38940..4270c7f0 100644
--- a/src/ModelingData/TKG2d/Geom2d/Geom2d_BezierCurve.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingData/TKG2d/Geom2d/Geom2d_BezierCurve.cxx
@@ -196,7 +196,8 @@
 {
   int nbpoles = NbPoles();
 
-  Standard_ConstructionError_Raise_if(nbpoles >= Geom2d_BezierCurve::MaxDegree()
+  // MaxDegree() bounds the degree, so MaxDegree() + 1 poles are legal, as the constructors allow.
+  Standard_ConstructionError_Raise_if(nbpoles > Geom2d_BezierCurve::MaxDegree()
                                         || Weight <= gp::Resolution(),
                                       "Geom2d_BezierCurve::InsertPoleAfter");
 
diff --git a/src/ModelingData/TKG3d/Geom/Geom_BezierCurve.cxx b/src/ModelingData/TKG3d/Geom/Geom_BezierCurve.cxx
index c6e4bd3a..ba5b4d5f 100644
--- a/src/ModelingData/TKG3d/Geom/Geom_BezierCurve.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingData/TKG3d/Geom/Geom_BezierCurve.cxx
@@ -211,7 +211,8 @@
 {
   int nbpoles = NbPoles();
 
-  if (nbpoles >= Geom_BezierCurve::MaxDegree() || Weight <= gp::Resolution())
+  // MaxDegree() bounds the degree, so MaxDegree() + 1 poles are legal, as the constructors allow.
+  if (nbpoles > Geom_BezierCurve::MaxDegree() || Weight <= gp::Resolution())
   {
     throw Standard_ConstructionError("Geom_BezierCurve::InsertPoleAfter");
   }
